{0 Liblevenshtein for OCaml}

This package performs spelling correction and fuzzy dictionary search with
Levenshtein-family automata. It consumes a dictionary resource produced by the
independently packaged [libdictenstein] OCaml library. The public module is
{!module:Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein}.

{1 First query}

The following code uses public modules only. Each owned native handle is
released even if an operation raises an exception.

{[
module D = Vinary_tree_libdictenstein
module L = Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein

let matches () =
  let dictionary = D.dynamic_dawg () in
  Fun.protect ~finally:(fun () -> D.close dictionary) (fun () ->
    ignore (D.put dictionary "cat" (Some 1L));
    ignore (D.put dictionary "cot" None);
    let transducer = L.transducer (D.resource dictionary) in
    Fun.protect ~finally:(fun () -> L.close_transducer transducer) (fun () ->
      let cursor = L.query transducer "cat" ~maximum_distance:1 in
      Fun.protect ~finally:(fun () -> L.cursor_close cursor) (fun () ->
        List.of_seq (L.to_seq cursor))))
]}

The query captures one immutable dictionary revision. Later inserts or
removals do not change an existing cursor. A match's [id = None] means that
the term is present without an associated value; it does not mean the term
is absent.

{1 Choosing a query domain and algorithm}

For UTF-8 text, use {!Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein.query}. For arbitrary bytes,
use a byte-domain dictionary and {!Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein.query_bytes};
embedded zero bytes are ordinary data. For unsigned 64-bit token arrays, use
an unsigned-64 dictionary and {!Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein.query_u64}; OCaml
[int64] values preserve the underlying unsigned bit patterns.

The default algorithm is standard Levenshtein distance. The transposition
variant uses optimal string alignment, which is not a metric. The unrestricted
Damerau-Levenshtein variant is distinct and can be used where metric laws
matter. Select with [L.transducer ~algorithm:L.Damerau_levenshtein resource].
Use [~order:L.Distance_then_term] to order matches by distance and term.

{1 Streaming and bounded caching}

The lazy {!Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein.to_seq} adapter does not close its cursor.
Always scope it with [Fun.protect], including when consumption ends early.
For fewer foreign-boundary crossings, use
{!Vinary_tree_liblevenshtein.fold_batches}; its arrays contain owned OCaml
matches. For repeated queries, [L.query_cache]
places hard bounds on resident entry count and weight. A cache eviction
changes performance only; results remain derivable from the captured revision.

{1 Errors and further reading}

Native failures raise OCaml [Failure] with a copied diagnostic. This facade
does not expose the numeric status as a typed exception. Check the relevant
operation and diagnostic; do not parse message text as a stable protocol.
See the package README for supported backend combinations and the native ABI
reference for status and threshold contracts:
https://github.com/vinary-tree/liblevenshtein-rust/blob/master/docs/bindings/c-abi-reference.md
